  "query_preview": "..\n\nVery strong H5 599 dam mid-upper level high pressure over Southeast \nUS will slowly retrograde to portions of east Texas and OK by \nFriday. The upper level jet axis over eastern Canada will be slow to \nmove which will keep the baroclinic zone in relatively close \nproximity to Southeast Michigan through at least the end of the week \nand beginning of the upcoming weekend.\n\nFor today, plan view perspective of thetae shows the near surface \nextension of the frontal zone slipping southward to the Ohio border \nwhile the 900-700mb frontal surface remains farther north between \nthe I 96-I 69 corridors. Differences exist with regards to the north \nsouth extent of shower/convective development later today. Consensus \nof many of the models including NAMnest and HRDPS initiates deep \nconvection to the south of IN/OH state line, whereas the ECMWF \nbrings QPF (from weaker elevated showers) much father north through \nmuch of Southeast Michigan. System relative flow trajectories \nhighlight a coherent 925-850mb cyclonic circulation emerging today \nnear South Bend that will track eastward increasing convergence. \nModels suggest shower activity approaching after 14z with spotty \nactivity in an around the area this afternoon. Out of respect for \nthe elevated portion of the frontal zone and the progged \nconvergence, will keep inherited broad brushed PoPs.\n\nDespite little north to south movement of the upper level jet axis, \nthe entrance region and confluence aloft will support stable surface \nhigh pressure late Thursday and Friday. Strong likelihood that \nincreasing anticyclonic flow trajectories will result in lower \ndewpoints into the 50s. A comfortable couple of days.\n\nRight entrance region to very large jet entrance brings the \npotential for upstream convection to ride eastward through Lower \nMichigan. There has been a subset of solutions that have a MCS \ndeveloping over the Plains states Friday and arriving over Southeast \nMichigan midday Saturday. Breadth of synoptic scale forcing with \ntime of year suggests heavy rainfall potential. The latest Excessive \nRainfall Outlook products have been highlighting a Slight risk for a \ngood portion of Southeast Michigan. Given the structure an \norganization of the thetae ridge a wave will act upon, the setup \nlooks good. The current forecast as 80-90% PoPs for Saturday. \nAnother trough structure does look to suppress heights and cause a \ncold front to slip southward for Sunday.",
